WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:05.160
In this video, we're going to talk about how to set up

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.080
two-way communication so the receiver can

3
00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.400
also send message back to the sender.

4
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.039
Now, to do that,

5
00:00:13.039 --> 00:00:17.000
let's take a look at what it is being set up right now.

6
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.519
The sender, you will see

7
00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:23.379
the two-way communication related part

8
00:00:23.379 --> 00:00:28.379
are all being commented in the yellow comment box.

9
00:00:28.379 --> 00:00:31.420
So first, at begin play,

10
00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:33.780
we will check if this instance

11
00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.340
should be in two-way communication.

12
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.759
If it is two-way for the sender,

13
00:00:38.759 --> 00:00:41.380
remember that before that the sender only

14
00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:44.780
has a OSC client targeting the receiver,

15
00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.100
so the sender doesn't have any way to receive messages.

16
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:53.159
So to allow the sender to also able to receive messages,

17
00:00:53.159 --> 00:00:56.679
we want the sender to create an OSC server.

18
00:00:56.679 --> 00:01:02.429
Now, because I know that my Internet

19
00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:06.510
is fairly simple, I'm going to just leave this part blank,

20
00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:13.540
so it will try to grab the first IP it has on this device.

21
00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:16.219
Remember to use the same port

22
00:01:16.219 --> 00:01:19.620
that is going to send the message back in.

23
00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:24.000
Right now, we just used OSC port variable,

24
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.620
which we can set in the launch parameter later.

25
00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:32.140
Now, I set the name of the OSC server to also be server.

26
00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.560
If you remember, on the receiver side,

27
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.219
we also have the same server name here.

28
00:01:38.219 --> 00:01:39.700
It's a bad practice,

29
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.480
so we actually should try to name them something else.

30
00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:45.959
But if you remember,

31
00:01:45.959 --> 00:01:48.340
a sender is never a receiver,

32
00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:53.579
so usually there will not be

33
00:01:53.579 --> 00:02:00.819
the same named OSC server component at the same instance.

34
00:02:00.859 --> 00:02:03.769
That is actually fine.

35
00:02:03.769 --> 00:02:05.650
But still, if we want to make sure

36
00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:08.409
the server name is never the same,

37
00:02:08.409 --> 00:02:14.360
we can also use sender IP address,

38
00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:17.080
for example, to be the name.

39
00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:21.960
So unless there's same instance from the same IP,

40
00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:25.240
the name will never be duplicated.

41
00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:30.319
So now we need the sender's own server as a different variable,

42
00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:33.789
and we want the server to react to

43
00:02:33.789 --> 00:02:37.750
all the event in the same way the receiver receives it.

44
00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:41.870
So now we want to bind it to the OSC boundary received,

45
00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:45.909
and also bind it to OSC message received.

46
00:02:45.909 --> 00:02:49.990
So when that happened,

47
00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:51.710
we actually wanted to just do

48
00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:54.389
the same thing as what the receiver is doing.

49
00:02:54.389 --> 00:02:56.430
So remember at begin play,

50
00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:02.270
we are getting the receiver on the same level as a reference.

51
00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:05.960
We're now going to get that reference and

52
00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:09.520
just fire the same event in OSC receiver.

53
00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:14.710
That's it. Now we make sure the sender also has

54
00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:18.349
a server is able to listening to combing OSC messages,

55
00:03:18.349 --> 00:03:21.530
and whenever there is a message or a bundle combing,

56
00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:24.150
it is doing the same reaction

57
00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:27.150
we wanted to do on the receiver side.

58
00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:30.479
Now let's go to our receiver side

59
00:03:30.479 --> 00:03:33.840
and move on to the two-way part.

60
00:03:33.879 --> 00:03:38.479
At begin play, it is checking if it is a two-way communication.

61
00:03:38.479 --> 00:03:41.960
If it is, it want to also be able to point

62
00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:46.919
to the sender and send message back to the sender.

63
00:03:46.919 --> 00:03:51.900
So for that, we want to create an OSC client.

64
00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.580
The OSC client will have to target to

65
00:03:54.580 --> 00:04:00.139
the sender IP address where we can set inside launch parameter.

66
00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:03.620
The same, we're going to use the OSC port here.

67
00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:11.000
We're going to save the client as a OSC client.

68
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.520
On the sender side,

69
00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.860
we want to save the client and we also

70
00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:24.579
want to save it as a variable on the OSC sender blueprint.

71
00:04:24.579 --> 00:04:30.589
Now, in order to send the messages,

72
00:04:30.589 --> 00:04:36.009
just so that we don't need to duplicate all the events,

73
00:04:36.009 --> 00:04:44.759
again, we will go back to our sender part.

74
00:04:44.759 --> 00:04:50.639
Now, if we go to the send messages event here,

75
00:04:50.639 --> 00:04:54.740
we will see that when we're sending the message,

76
00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:59.240
we are selecting our target based

77
00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:04.480
on if the instance being played is a sender.

78
00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.399
Now, if I am a sender,

79
00:05:06.399 --> 00:05:11.160
I know the OSC client I want to target is the first one.

80
00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:13.680
Because this is the first one I saved

81
00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:17.160
before I do the two-way communication thing,

82
00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:20.680
this is the receiver's target.

83
00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:25.980
But if the instance being played is not a sender,

84
00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:27.259
it is a receiver,

85
00:05:27.259 --> 00:05:30.740
then we know receiver want to use this target.

86
00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:33.100
It's targeting the sender.

87
00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:35.540
Now, this might feels a little bit complicated,

88
00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:41.579
but just know that we are just reusing the same set of logic.

89
00:05:41.579 --> 00:05:44.139
Based on who is sending,

90
00:05:44.139 --> 00:05:50.500
we will target destinations here.

91
00:05:50.899 --> 00:05:54.220
We know that this part is already taken care

92
00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:57.459
of for the example events here.

93
00:05:57.459 --> 00:06:00.379
We just want to grab this logic,

94
00:06:00.379 --> 00:06:07.490
we will copy and put into the new two events we just created.

95
00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:12.930
We know that OSC client here,

96
00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:15.769
so we know that the send event here is working for

97
00:06:15.769 --> 00:06:18.129
one-way communication to make

98
00:06:18.129 --> 00:06:20.449
it working on two-way communication.

99
00:06:20.449 --> 00:06:30.300
I will replace the target to be this one.

100
00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:32.860
Same thing for the bundle,

101
00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:35.740
I'm just going to drag down here,

102
00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:43.180
delete this node, like so.

103
00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:46.500
Now we're ready to run the two-way testing.

104
00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:49.019
Now just before we make a package,

105
00:06:49.019 --> 00:06:54.620
I'll just connect back to where it should be,

106
00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:57.779
so make sure we are using the launch parameter,

107
00:06:57.779 --> 00:07:03.670
like so, save it.

108
00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:06.790
Now we already finished

109
00:07:06.829 --> 00:07:09.350
working on the two-way communication.

110
00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:13.829
Now let's move on to package this project.
